<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hastings Banda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1898–<a href="/years/1997">1997</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Hastings Kamuzu Banda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hastings Kamuzu Banda was a Malawian physician and politician who led Nyasaland to independence as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a> in 1964. He returned home in 1958 to lead the campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</a> and was detained during the 1959 emergency. As prime minister and from 1966 president, declared president for life in 1971, he ruled a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> until he lost multiparty elections in 1994.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hastings-banda.jpg" alt="Hastings Banda" width="320" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Archives of Malawi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dr_HK_Banda,_first_president_of_Malawi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hastings Kamuzu Banda was born Akim Kamnkhwala Mtunthama Banda near Kasungu, to Mphonongo Banda and Akupingamnyama Phiri. No birth was registered, so his date of birth is unknown. After attending village and mission schools he was baptised in 1910. He studied at the Wilberforce Institute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, graduating from its high school section in 1928, and later at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, which awarded him the Scottish Triple Qualification in 1941.</p><p>He became an elder of a parish in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-scotland">Church of Scotland</a>. In 1951 he moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>, and Malawian Congress leaders, among them Henry Chipembere and Kanyama Chiume, later asked him to return and lead their cause.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Banda opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</a>, formed in 1953, because he feared it would cost Nyasaland's Africans further rights. He reached Nyasaland on 6 July 1958 after an absence of about 42 years and toured the country speaking against the federation. By February 1959 Rhodesian troops had been flown in and a state of emergency declared. On 3 March he was arrested in Operation Sunrise and held in Gwelo in Southern Rhodesia.</p><p>He was released in April 1960 and invited to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> for talks on independence. After elections in August 1961 he became de facto prime minister, a title formally granted on 1 February 1963. In December 1962 the British Secretary of State R. A. Butler essentially agreed to end the federation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>A month after independence Banda dismissed four ministers who had proposed limits on his powers, and the dissidents fled the country. The constitution of 6 July 1966 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a> a republic and Banda its first president, the only candidate, and formally made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi-congress-party">Malawi Congress Party</a> the sole legal party. The party made him president for life in 1970, and the legislature did the same for the country in 1971. His title included the word Ngwazi, meaning saviour or conqueror in Chichewa.</p><p>In 1983 three ministers and a member of parliament who had favoured multiparty politics died in what was officially a traffic accident. Adult citizens had to carry party cards, the Malawi Young Pioneers acted as the party's paramilitary wing, and films and books were censored. Human rights groups estimate that at least 6,000 people were killed, tortured or jailed without trial. With the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, donors pressed for reform, and in 1993 Banda agreed to a referendum that ended the one-party system. He was defeated in the elections that followed and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> on 25 November 1997.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hastings Banda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its delegates, among them Jomo Kenyatta, <b>Hastings Banda</b> and Obafemi Awolowo, and representatives of African trade unions and student groups demanded independence for Africa.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Nyasaland became independent from British rule on 6 July 1964 and was renamed Malawi, with <b>Hastings Banda</b>, leader of the Malawi Congress Party (MCP), as prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/state/malawi">Malawi</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hastings Kamuzu Banda</b> returned in 1958, a state of emergency followed in 1959, and his Malawi Congress Party won the 1961 elections.</blockquote><a href="/state/nyasaland">Nyasaland</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kasungu, in what is now <a href="/countries/malawi">Malawi</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-13.03,33.49&amp;year=1962" aria-label="Kasungu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Hastings Banda"><image href="/maps/globes/g03000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5290 6105h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5290 6105h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-13.03,33.49&amp;year=1962">What was here in 1962? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
